 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:58:55.952279Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable as written**, because of its 30-day notice period.\n\nSection 116 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* governs rent increases. It allows a landlord to increase rent by no more than the guideline, and only once in a 12-month period. The clause's cap and frequency terms track those limits, so they are not the problem. The problem is the notice period: s. 116 requires written notice, on the prescribed form (N1), at least 90 days before the increase takes effect. A 30-day notice falls short of that. Under s. 4, the Act applies despite any contrary agreement, so the landlord cannot contract for a shorter period. The landlord could still impose a guideline increase, but only on proper 90-day notice.\n\nI have cited s. 116 as the governing section from memory and have not checked the subsection numbering against the current consolidation, so verify the exact subsection before relying on it.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 116",
